= Law of diminishing returns =

== Overview ==

{{:Law of diminishing returns/Simple}}

The '''law of diminishing returns''' (often '''diminishing marginal returns''' in production theory) concerns how the marginal contribution of one variable production input changes while other relevant inputs and the technology are fixed. In a differentiable one-input slice of a production function <math>Q=f(L,\bar K)</math> for labor <math>L</math> and fixed capital <math>\bar K</math>, positive but diminishing marginal product means

<math display="block">\frac{\partial Q}{\partial L}>0,\qquad\frac{\partial^2Q}{\partial L^2}<0</math>

over the stated input range. The first inequality allows total output to rise while the second says the slope falls. Neither follows from a production increase by itself.

== Scope and confusions ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Term !! What changes !! Important distinction
|-
| Diminishing marginal product || Additional output per extra unit of one variable input, holding others fixed || Not necessarily declining total product
|-
| Negative marginal product || Additional input lowers total output || Stronger condition than diminishing positive returns
|-
| Returns to scale || All relevant productive inputs multiplied together || Can be constant even when each input's marginal product diminishes
|-
| Marginal utility || A consumer's additional utility from consumption || Preference theory, not production technology
|}

Standard economics texts describe diminishing marginal productivity under short-run production constraints rather than a statement about all growing organizations or every mathematical system.<ref name="openstax_core">OpenStax, [https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/7-2-production-in-the-short-run ''Principles of Economics'', §7.2: Production in the Short Run], definitions, marginal product and the role of fixed inputs.</ref>

A production experiment needs consistent output units, fixed nonlabor inputs, a time period and attention to learning, worker selection, quality and management. A change in observed output may reflect changes in the environment rather than the added resource.

== Models and application ==

The Cobb–Douglas family can illustrate the distinction between individual marginal products and scale changes, but it is not evidence that all industries have identical elasticity or that production is always concave. A complete economic conclusion also requires prices, technology, input substitutability and quality measurement.

Related areas include [[Related to::Scientific model|scientific modeling]], [[Related to::Computational complexity|resource analysis]] and [[Related to::Robustness|sensitivity to resource constraints]]. [[Law of diminishing returns/Technical|Technical]] independently computes a concrete production example.

